  <constraints>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 5 + Q5">Scope: every root (project, matter, work assignment) and every registry-listed Contact- or Organization-typed column on it. The auto level is COLLABORATE (`ExternalAccessLevel` Collaborate for grants; `RecordShareLevels` Collaborate for shares). Take the mask from `RecordShareLevels`, never a new constant. If another task adds ShareAccess to Collaborate (C4), that must flow through unchanged.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 5 (operator removal must stick)">Scope: /revoke, /unshare-user, the materializer and the job. Removing an auto-created grant or share through Manage Access records Declined for that (subject, root, source field). No trigger may re-create a Declined entry while that assignment persists. Declined is NOT a No Access entry: the operator may still grant the same subject manually.
- OUT-OF-BAND removal sticks too. An auto grant row deactivated outside the BFF (MDA form or grid, Web API, flow) or an auto share removed through the OOB MDA Share dialog is detected by the sync and the job (ledger Granted/Shared but the grant/share is gone, and no BFF-known cause) and recorded Declined, never re-created.
- Removal by a KNOWN cause is not Declined: a grant that lapsed by expiry, or was deactivated by `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob` R2 (inactive organization), follows escalation (e); a share removed by task 143's No Access enforcer is Skipped(no-access), so the Assigned invariant re-creates it once the wall is lifted.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11 reuse">Scope: every write this task makes. Grant rows go through `GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.CreateGrantAsync` (`:186`) and revokes through `ExternalGrantLifecycle.DeactivateAsync` (`:406`), so expiry, dedupe, cache invalidation and the FR-32 choke point (task 087) apply. POA shares go through `IDataverseRecordShareService` with the strict read + read-back discipline of `InternalShareEndpoints.ShareAsync`. Systemuser eligibility reuses `EligibilityRefusal`'s rule. Secure/Restricted flags come from `ExternalParticipationService.GetRootRecordFlagsAsync` (`:541`). Registry columns come from the bound `MembershipOptions.AccessConferringRoles` — never a second hardcoded list.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner (binding directive)">Scope: auto grants. An absent expiry becomes today + 90 days through the existing `DefaultExpiry`; the materializer never passes an unbounded or client-chosen date. Renewal behaviour follows the owner's answer to escalation (e).</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 3 (Restricted / Secure)">Scope: materializer decisions, evaluated through task 138's write-time access-permission rule (never a private copy).
- Restricted → no contact grant and no organization grant. POA shares for linked internal systemusers are unaffected.
- Secure → no organization grant.
- Secure → contact grants and POA shares follow escalation (a).
- A flag that cannot be read → no write this pass, recorded as Skipped(flags-unreadable). ADR-003: fail closed.</constraint>
    <constraint source="FR-23 / Q4">Scope: materializer decisions.
- A contact subject on the No Access List for the record (contact or organization subject; record or referenced-organization object, via `NoAccessListReader`) gets no grant, recorded as Skipped(no-access).
- For the POA branch on a secure record, call task 143's check.
- An unreadable deny list is a skip, never a grant.</constraint>
    <constraint source="never lower access">Scope: materializer. An existing ACTIVE grant or share at an equal or higher level, or with a later expiry, is left untouched (recorded CoveredByExisting). The materializer never downgrades a level, never shortens an expiry, and never revokes a manually created or Adopted grant/share.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003 / ADR-008 — sync endpoint">Scope: `POST .../assigned-access/sync`.
- It joins the existing `/api/v1/external-access` admin group, so `DelegationRuleFilter` (Write on the record, as the caller) gates it. Add a case for the sync request type to the filter's target switch (`DelegationRuleFilter.cs:211-281`) that resolves the record through `GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.ResolveExplicitRoot`, as the `/share-user` case does (`:264-265`). Prove it with a test through the real filter pipeline that a caller WITH Write reaches the handler — a filter test alone, or a handler test that bypasses the filter, does not meet this.
- The body carries ONLY recordType + recordId. Subjects are read server-side from the record; any client-supplied subject is ignored and never trusted.
- Every refusal or fault is ProblemDetails with a stable reason code and a human message, never a bare 500.</constraint>
    <constraint source="C9 plane parity — cache">Scope: every POA share the materializer writes or removes, from ANY trigger. The target systemuser's impersonated root-set cache is invalidated under the SAME tenant key `ImpersonatedRootSetSource.GetAsync` reads. In the job (no `HttpContext`, no caller) that key comes from the deployment's tenant configuration, never `CacheTenantFor(null)` = "anonymous". A test asserts the removed key equals the key a read for that user would use.</constraint>
    <constraint source="WP-2 / WP-3 (DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE.md) — client writers">Scope: every CLIENT census writer that creates or updates a root with a registry column (the Create Matter wizard at minimum). After its write succeeds it calls the sync endpoint with the new record's type and id (through the host's existing authenticated fetch), so wizard-created assignees get access at creation, not at the next job tick. A failed sync call is logged and never fails or rolls back the wizard; the job is the safety net.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-1 / ADR-002 / ADR-052 / ADR-036">Scope: triggers. No Dataverse plugin, flow or service-endpoint step. The job is an `IScheduledJob` registered with `AddScheduledJob` in `ExternalAccessModule`, following `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob`'s A1 rules: claim → check → write chunk → marker; record failure (`Success=false`), never "nothing to reconcile"; no throw from `ExecuteAsync` unless a retry this tick can finish lost work. Its enabled/write posture follows escalation (g).</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §6.5 path B">Scope: this task's PR. Materializing derived access contradicts spec.md:163 ("MUST NOT materialize derived access into grant rows"), spec FR-32 (:118) and design.md:685. It also changes ADR-034 A1's read-time conferral model and FR-25's standing-grant semantics. Draft the amendments and put the path-B block in the PR description (see adr-tensions). The code merges alongside the amendments, never before.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner directives (binding)">Scope: whole task.
- ADR-028 A4: no new `.WithClientSecret(...)`.
- `SpeContainerMembershipService.GrantMembershipAsync` stays at zero callers.
- `TargetEntity` is never made required on document saves.
- The SPE-item-id alternate key is not relaxed.
- D-13 per-customer BFF app registration is untouched.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR (BFF-touching).
- State the placement decision against `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md`: BFF, beside the other external-access writers.
- Measure publish size against a FRESH master worktree and a fresh branch worktree, both at SHORT paths, zipped with Compress-Archive. Report both sizes, the delta and both file counts. Ceiling 60 MB.
- `dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive` shows no new HIGH.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests. No `Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;`, no DI-registration tests, no constructor null-check tests. Use the repo's subclass-override seams (as `NoAccessListReader.QueryChunkAsync` and `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob` tests do).</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner (binding)">Scope: verification. No Dataverse test in CI. Live behaviour is proven by a MANUAL gate on spaarkedev1 using the existing non-admin test users in their current BUs. Do not relocate users.</constraint>
      <constraint source="main-session-review">Persist every auto-grant through 139's grant core with an explicit, documented Collaborate ceiling (owner Q5) — never by writing sprk_externalrecordaccess directly — so the never-lower rule and 138's refusal codes apply: record_restricted is skip-and-log (no grant), and an existing grant at a higher level is never lowered. Depends on 139.</constraint>
</constraints>

  <adr-tensions>
    <tension path="B" adr="ADR-034 A1 + spec.md:163 / FR-32 / FR-25 + design.md §7:685">Rule challenged: "MUST NOT materialize derived access into grant rows" and the read-time derived-member model. Conflict: the owner (round 2 Q5) requires the Assigned-field access to be a removable entry on the grant-access list. A read-time term cannot be removed by an operator without a veto, and a veto is the No Access List, which is a different, stronger statement. Proposed path: B, amending ADR-034 (conferral for contacts becomes materialized, removable grants maintained by the invariant owner), spec (strike the MUST NOT for Assigned-field access; FR-25 per escalation (d)), and design §7 (auto grants are grant state changes, logged by FR-32's event log). Alternative considered: A, a project-scoped exception. Rejected because the rule is binding repo-wide via ADR-034 and every future reader would meet the contradiction. Alternative considered: C, keep the read-time term and add a per-record "declined" veto. Rejected because it is a second deny mechanism beside the No Access List (§11), and on MDA the term is invisible, so MDA and SPA would disagree (C9).</tension>
  </adr-tensions>

  <justification>
    <existing>(1) Invariant owner: nothing materializes access from Assigned fields. `rg "sprk_assignedattorney1" src/server --type cs` finds the registry (MembershipOptions.cs:305+), read paths and the field-mapping seeder. No writer creates grants from them. `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob` (R1-R3) repairs grant/membership row lifecycle and does not derive grants. (2) Ledger: `sprk_externalrecordaccess` (grants only, no provenance column, one row per subject×root shared with manual grants), `sprk_userentityassociation` (docs/data-model/sprk_userentityassociation.md:79-88 — person×record×sourcefield, owned by the ADR-034 membership pipeline), `sprk_noaccessentry` (a veto). (3) Sync endpoint: no existing route derives access from a record; `/grant` takes client-chosen subjects. (4) Web resource: `sprk_kpiassessment_quickcreate.js` is the only OnPostSave→BFF caller, and it is KPI-specific.</existing>
    <extension>(1) Extending `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob` with an R4 was rejected: that job's contract is "a row's own statecode/expiry is the truth" (:16-19). It ships disabled/report-only under D-2 part 3, which would switch off a feature the owner requires. The materializer itself reuses CreateGrantAsync/DeactivateAsync/share seam (extension, not fork). (2) The ledger cannot extend `sprk_externalrecordaccess`: a POA share has no row, and a manual /grant upserts onto the same row, which destroys provenance. It cannot extend `sprk_userentityassociation` either: `MembershipReconciliationJob` deletes orphan rows when an assignment clears — exactly when revoke-on-change needs the provenance. Its writers are also flag-off by default. (3) The endpoint extends the existing delegation-filtered group rather than a new group. (4) The web resource reuses `sprk_bff_auth.js` and cannot be the KPI script, which does unrelated work.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>(1) When 036's flag turns on, an internal attorney named in `sprk_assignedattorney1` (via their linked contact) loses Teams/SPA access to the matter. An external contact named there has access only if it holds a standing grant, contradicting owner Q5. (2) Without a ledger: an operator who removes an auto grant sees it re-created by the next job run (owner: removal must stick). Changing the Assigned field cannot tell an auto grant from a manual grant on the same row, so it either revokes access someone granted deliberately or leaves the previous assignee with access. (3) Without the form post-save call: MDA edits — the dominant writer — wait up to a full job cadence for access. (4) Without the job: grid edits, imports and flows never receive access, and a form or wizard whose sync call failed is never repaired. (5) Without the wizard's post-create sync call: every wizard-created matter's assignees wait a full job cadence for access.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

