  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Confirm 141, 138, 139 and 143 are merged, re-deriving each one's delivered surface from its own POML/notes. Obtain owner answers to escalation triggers (a), (b), (c), (d), (e) and (g) BEFORE implementing those branches, and record the answers in notes/task-142-assigned-field-auto-grants.md.</step>
    <step order="1">Inventory. List every registry column per root from the BOUND registry and verify each against live metadata (Dataverse MCP describe). Record the child-entity registry entries and their disposition per escalation (f). If any live Assigned-type lookup on a root is missing from the registry, fire escalation (h).</step>
    <step order="2">Writer census. Find every BFF path that creates or updates a root with a registry column: at least `RecordCreationService` and `UpdateRecordActionCore`. Find every client path: wizards and field-mapping profiles targeting a root. Record each with file:line and its trigger (L1 inline / form post-save / job).</step>
    <step order="3">Create the `sprk_assignedaccess` ledger under the Spaarke publisher in SpaarkeCore. Shape to finalize: one typed root lookup (project/matter/workassignment, as `sprk_externalrecordaccess` does), `sprk_sourcefield`, subject contact / organization / systemuser lookups, grant lookup, state choice (Granted, Shared, CoveredByExisting, PendingConfirmation, Skipped, Declined, Adopted, Revoked), skip reason, and uniqueness on (root, source field, subject). Because the root and the subject are each one of three NULLABLE typed lookups, do not assume a Dataverse alternate key over those lookups enforces uniqueness correctly: either verify live that a key over them rejects a duplicate and accepts two rows that differ only in which lookup is null, or key on a single BFF-computed string column (e.g. `{roottype}:{rootid}:{sourcefield}:{subjecttype}:{subjectid}`). Only the BFF app user may create, update or delete rows: no Spaarke user security role gets Create/Write/Delete on the table (verify the live role privileges, record them); users get Read only if Manage Access reads the ledger directly. Write the schema doc and verify live.</step>
    <step order="4">Build the materializer. For one root:
- read the registry columns and the flags;
- resolve each subject — contact linked via 141 to an eligible systemuser → POA share; contact otherwise → grant; organization → org grant;
- apply the veto/flag rules;
- reconcile against the ledger (create, skip, cover, or revoke per escalation (b));
- write the ledger.
It must be idempotent: an unchanged record means zero Dataverse writes.</step>
    <step order="5">Wire the markers. `/revoke` of a row the ledger holds as Granted → Declined. `/unshare-user` of a Shared user → Declined. A manual `/grant` or `/share-user` onto a ledger-held subject → Adopted (never auto-revoked afterwards). Out-of-band disappearance (grant row deactivated outside the BFF, share removed through the OOB MDA dialog) → Declined; known causes (expiry, R2, task 143's enforcer) per the operator-removal constraint.</step>
    <step order="6">Wire the triggers:
- L1 inline calls in every census BFF writer, after the write commits (a materializer fault after a successful create is logged and left to the job; it never fails the create);
- the sync endpoint, plus its `DelegationRuleFilter` case;
- every client census writer calls the sync endpoint after a successful create/update;
- the web resource on the three main forms (deploy via the dataverse-deploy skill; the form-library registration order puts sprk_bff_auth.js first);
- the reconciliation job, covering the full root sweep, 141 link changes (convert grant → share, carrying Declined), and Secure/Restricted/No-Access transitions.</step>
    <step order="7">Apply the owner's answers: (a) Secure branch incl. the Manage Access candidate/suggestion surface if "prompt"; (b) revoke-on-change; (d) retire or keep the read-time standing/org-expansion terms, with the backfill run BEFORE any retirement so no subject loses access in between; (e) renewal; (g) job posture.</step>
    <step order="8">Tests per the acceptance test-scope clause; build; ArchTests; publish-size and CVE checks per §10.</step>
    <step order="9">Docs: add the invariant row to DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE.md §5; draft the ADR-034 full-ADR amendment and the spec/design amendment text; hand the concise `.claude/adr` edit to the main session.</step>
    <step order="10">MANUAL live gate on spaarkedev1 (acceptance criterion 21). Record the evidence in the notes file. Then set the job's revoke-on-change switch to the owner's answer to (g) in the dev configuration as part of THIS task, and record the setting.</step>
    <step order="11">Update TASK-INDEX.md: this task's cell to `✅ [done]`. Record deviations in projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>(a) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — Secure records. Should the materializer auto-create the contact grant / POA share on a SECURE root, or prompt the operator? RECOMMENDATION: prompt. Secure records are deliberately tightly controlled, and today their internal access is only explicit shares. Implement the prompt as ledger state PendingConfirmation, surfaced in the EXISTING Manage Access candidate section (server-derived from the registry, replacing `CANDIDATE_ROLE_FIELDS`) with Grant / Dismiss. Dismiss = Declined. Also decide what happens to existing auto grants when a record BECOMES secure. Recommendation: keep them; they are direct named grants, which count on secure records and are visible and removable in Manage Access. STOP before implementing the Secure branch until answered.</trigger>
    <trigger>(b) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — Assigned field changed or cleared. Revoke the previous subject's auto grant/share if it is still the unmodified auto-created one? RECOMMENDATION: yes. "Unmodified" = ledger state Granted/Shared, level and expiry unchanged since creation (except by the materializer's own renewal), not Adopted, and no OTHER registry column on the same root still names the subject. Declined and Adopted entries are never revoked by this rule.</trigger>
    <trigger>(c) RULE CONFLICT to confirm — Q1 "cap every grant at the grantor's own level" vs Q5 "automatically Collaborate". Both answers are BINDING, so this is not the implementer's choice to resolve — confirm before any auto grant is written. RECOMMENDATION: Q5 governs auto grants, because the grantor is the owner's rule, not a person. Setting an Assigned field already requires Write on the record, so in practice the saver holds Read|Write; the one divergence is ShareAccess once task 139 adds it to Collaborate, which a saver without the Share privilege does not hold — name that case in the question. `sprk_grantedby` = the user whose save triggered the sync (so FR-33 reminders reach a person, `GrantExpiryReminderJob.cs:24-30`), or empty for job-created grants (reminders then go to the record owner).</trigger>
    <trigger>(d) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — does materialization REPLACE the read-time contact terms? These are the standing-grant membership term (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1147-1175`, FR-25) and org expansion (`:1177-1249`, FR-24/task 043). Keeping both means two mechanisms and an auto grant that an operator "removes" while standing access silently remains. Retiring them changes FR-25: a View Only standing contact would receive Collaborate, and org expansion was standing-gated where the org auto grant is not. RECOMMENDATION: retire both from the access composition after the backfill proves every subject they served now holds an equal-or-higher materialized grant. Record the amendment. The live data is 2 standing contacts at Collaborate and 0 standing orgs (TASK-INDEX row 042).</trigger>
    <trigger>(e) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — expiry of auto grants. The binding +90-day default means a long matter's assignee loses access at day 90. RECOMMENDATION: the job renews an auto grant that is still assigned, not Declined, not Adopted and not modified, inside the FR-33 reminder window, to today + 90 through `DefaultExpiry`. Every row stays finite, and the feature does not lapse. The alternative is to let it expire and rely on reminders plus the existing renewal route.</trigger>
    <trigger>(f) Child-entity registry entries (event, invoice, todo, analysis) confer nothing on any access gate today. RECOMMENDATION: they create NO root grant, because a contact assigned to one event is not assigned to the matter. Confirm. If step 1 finds any gate composing a child entity type, STOP.</trigger>
    <trigger>(g) Job posture. ExternalAccessReconciliationJob ships disabled/report-only for REMOVAL rules (D-2 part 3). RECOMMENDATION: register this job ENABLED with writes on for create/convert/renew, and gate its revoke-on-change writes behind a positively named switch that defaults to report-only in code and is turned ON in dev by this task once the live gate (criterion 21) passes — so the removal direction is not left switched off with no owner of the flip. The switch governs the JOB only; the sync endpoint and the L1/client triggers always apply revoke-on-change per (b). Confirm the cadence (recommend hourly). While the switch is report-only, the fail-OPEN window for a non-product field change is unbounded rather than one cadence — say so in the PR.</trigger>
    <trigger>(h) A live Assigned-type lookup on a root that is NOT in the registry, or a registry column whose live target type disagrees. STOP: adding a conferring column is a reviewed registry edit (ADR-034 A1), not an implementation detail.</trigger>
    <trigger>(i) Any census writer that cannot call the materializer after commit without changing its API contract or its transaction. STOP rather than weaken the writer.</trigger>
    <trigger>(j) The live gate (criterion 21 (ii)) needs a linked internal test user with NO pre-existing Dataverse access to the chosen root. If no such user/root pair exists among the current test users in their current BUs, STOP and ask the owner which existing record/user pair to use. Do NOT relocate a user, and do NOT accept a pass on a root the user could already open.</trigger>
  </escalation>

