    <outcome date="2026-10-03" branch="task/uac-r2-142-r2" base="4ac362f8e" round="r2 (adversarial verifier, 16 items)">
FIX ROUND r2. Full per-item record: notes/task-142-assigned-field-auto-grants.md section 12; seeds in section 5 ("Round r2 seeds"); R-12 rewritten in section 9; the finding-3 deviation in section 6. Live writes: none.

FIXED. (1/11, medium-high; criterion 10, ADR-003) A raised MANUAL grant is now put back to the operator's level AND date. The raise records both in the ledger reason - raised-from:{level}@{yyyy-MM-dd} (AssignedAccessReason.RaisedFromLevel: the highest conferring level, the latest conferring date) - and the end of the assignment writes both through the grant core (explicit lower level, explicit date; not narrowed). The rule still renews a raised grant while it is assigned (rule 5 / A5), so access never outlives the assignment: when the operator's date has already passed, the restore ENDS the access - recorded Revoked(prior-level-restored-lapsed), action revoked (or ledger when the grant had already lapsed), and the grantee's grant-set cache is cleared by the materializer because the core skips its own invalidation on that warning path. The probe scenario (ViewOnly to today + 10, raised, renewed to today + 90, field cleared) now ends at ViewOnly with today + 10. The misleading comment at the covering check and the class remarks were corrected. A reason without the date (never written to a live ledger - the table is not deployed) takes the removal path (fail closed). (2/12, medium; criterion 16 / ADR-036 A1) The grant core's policy refusals of a restore - record_restricted, org_grant_direct_only_record, grantee_denied - are a HOLD, not a failure: outcome entry Granted / restore-pending:{reasonCode} / action none, zero writes, Complete stays true (job green; no HTTP 500 sync_incomplete; no false "retried within 5 minutes" warning). The row is deliberately NOT settled Revoked as suggested: a Revoked row is never revisited and the job's ledger scan skips all-Revoked roots, so the restore would be lost and the grant would confer at Collaborate again when the policy changed. The row stays Granted, every pass retries, and the first pass after the policy allows it restores level and date. policy_unreadable stays a failure. R-12 rewritten with the true scope (Restricted, an organization on Secure or Limited - possibly permanent - and the No Access list) and the residual (at most one pass at Collaborate after the policy relaxes; a form save or Update Access applies it at once, the job within 5 minutes). (3/13, low) Recorded as a deviation (notes section 6, code comment beside the write, pinning test): an EXPIRED Full Access row on the key is rewritten at Collaborate + today + 90 - the rule's ceiling (A1) cannot write Full, the core refuses a narrowed request over a higher row, and renewing at Full would grant above the rule's level; no access is lowered (none to Collaborate). (4, low) Status set to in-progress with a status-note naming exactly what is open (TASK-INDEX already reads open; not edited per the harness).

TESTS ADDED (9; each guard seeded alone by exact string replacement, watched fail, restored byte-for-byte with the MD5 re-checked, and touched): the probe scenario restored to level and date; renewed past the operator's date ends with the assignment, reported revoked, cache cleared; the policy-hold theory (organization on Secure / Limited / Restricted, contact on Restricted, contact on the No Access list - two passes complete with zero writes, then restored once allowed); the unreadable-policy twin (still a failure); the finding-3 deviation pin. Existing raise tests assert the new reason format and the restored date. Seeds: S6 restore level only 2; S7 raise records level only 10; S8 no cache invalidation on the warning path 1; S9 always ledger 1; S9b always revoked 1; S10 no hold 5; S11 every refusal a hold 1; S12 skip an expired grant above Collaborate 1.

